This diversity of subgroups implies different strategies for communication, and possibly different health interventions. For instance, the sexual orientation of participants differed markedly by gender (see @fig-sexorientation-racegender). The vast majority of cisgender men identified as gay, with some identifying as bisexual. Transgender men, on the other hand, were most likely to identify as queer, followed by bisexual or gay. Transgender women tended to identify as straight, bisexual, or other sexual orientations. Cisgender women participants tended to be straight, followed by bisexual, followed by queer. For non-binary people, most identified as queer, followed by gay and bisexual.


